A||||4||Lynx||Clutch removal.||||1||
Z||000000||Lynx||12-09-1999||01:14 AM||||Ok im finally changing the clutch in my 85 SC (4cyl) Ive got the rear in the air and have decided to pull it by removing the back driverside wheel and crap. I do not want to drop the whole thing. Will some one give me a good step by step process on how to pull the clutch and replace it that way please.<P>Lynx||||reg||1
Z||000001||ggarsk||12-09-1999||10:08 AM||||Lynx,Do a search for the info on changing your clutch. There are many threads in the forum on that.||||reg||1
Z||000002||HarryG||12-09-1999||12:35 PM||||Ed Parks of the Fiero Factory in Alabama posted a clutch procedure to another list a couple months ago. It was pretty detailed. Right now, I can't find that post, but maybe Ed saved a copy.<BR>Try him at &lt;fierofactory@juno.com&gt;  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Sage||12-10-1999||04:45 PM||||Have not done this myself but a very good mechanic friend of mine says he has done clutches in these cars by removing axles, splitting the tranny block/joint and pulling the trans back far enough to allow access to the clutch assembly.(you will have to remove tranny mounts as well I would assume.) I have found that as easy and quick as it is to pull the entire cradle, that is the most effective way to do anything major to the engine or transmission.  Just my opinion.  The hardest part of the whole operation is getting the cooling system filled properly!||||reg||1
Z||000004||dogtired||12-10-1999||05:30 PM||||Here ya go Lynx:<BR>And I am putting it here for others to enjoy.<BR>I just happened to have it handy. I have gotten great help from Ed and don't think he will mind me sharing his response here.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>From: fierofactory@juno.com  Jim, Jan, Calvin, Bits & Pieces and the Latest One, you don't have to drop the engine and cradle to remove the transaxle. You are going to ****  the cradle and remove the transaxle from under the left lower frame rail. No need to unwire the engine or even drain the coolant. However, you WILL need a special tool to make it easier.............a piece of 2X4 about 8" long! Also, jackstands and a floor jack.  Do the following from the top. Disconnect the battery, take the "S" air inlet tube off and detach the dogbone from either end. Remove the slave and it's heat shield and mount bracket, leave the line attached, and hang it out of the way. Take the shift cables, back up light, and VSS connections loose. Remove the exhaust crossover bolts to manifold and heat shields, disconnect the EGR valve pipe from the adapter (carefully or it WILL crack, may anyway).  After getting the car on jackstands, remove the starter to get the dustshield off. Then replace the starter (important part of the trick). On the drivers side remove wheel well, break the tierod from the spindle, remove the pinch bolt from the ball joint, take the emergency cable off the caliper, remove the caliper or the hydraulic line (we do that and bleed later), the three strut bolts from inside the engine compartment. and then using a prybar, "pop" the axle out of the trans. This allows removing the entire left suspension including the axle (except lower control arm) as a unit without messing up alignment when reinstalled and it leaves a "clean" space for the trans to come through.  Place floor jack (with piece of flat wood) under the engine oil pan for support/slight lift. Remove trans mounts and BRACKETS. Lower engine/trans onto "Special Tool 2X4" placed between starter and  engine cradle. Remove<BR>select cable bracket. Removing the various brackets gives less<BR>possibilities of catching the trans on anything.  Then place floor jack under the rear of cradle and remove the cradle bolts on pass side rear, and drs side front and rear.  Lower jack until necessary trans clearance is attained. This will kink the coolant hose but no harm should happen as the necessary clearance is just before that point.  Remove bellhousing bolts (remembering where the studded ones are, and that the lowest one toward the trunk is from the pass side). If you do not have enough clearance to remove the bolts, then remove the exhaust crossover from the downpipe.<BR>Remove trans axle. Should take about 1 1/2-2 1/2 hours. <BR>You will need an extra hand to reinstall. That is used to guide the pass<BR>axle into the trans. Should take 2-3 hrs.   Suggest you remove the clutch and inspect. Also remove the flywheel and reseal the cam cover (use black RTV, not a gasket). Rear main seal if leaking.<BR>This is basically what is required to change a clutch in 84-87 Fieros.  <BR>88's require an entirely different procedure, but still utilize the<BR>"special tool 2X4".  In your case, we suspect the imput bearing/release bearing collar has snapped off the Getrag 5 spd at the bellhousing. And if so, "Proper" way is to split the trans case and drive the bearing out from the inside. Rodney has the necessary pages, for only a few $, from the Pontiac manual explaining how to split the 5 spd case (88 was the only manual with instructions).   "Improper" way is to carefully cut the remaining bearing out with a dremel tool, flush the trans several times, and install a new imput bearing, all from the outside. Either way will take several hours.   $400+parts is not unreasonable, but, "+ whatever" may be very costly if<BR>they don't know what they are doing. The imput bearing is about $80<BR>retail. The rest is a lot of time/labor. <BR>Please save this to pass on to someone else. I am not sure that I<BR>remember how, and would hate to lose this much typing!<BR>Ed Parks, from The Fiero Factory 8710B Hwy 53, Toney, Ala 35773<BR>Shop: 256-420-5391...Hm: 256-882-1568 <A HREF="http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory" TARGET=_blank>http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory</A> <BR>On Fri, 12 Nov 1999 18:30:36 -0700 James W La Barge &lt;labarge1@juno.com&gt;<BR>writes:<BR>&gt; Ed, I may live to regret this, but I am not getting much <BR>&gt; encouragement asking repair places about doing the repairs...$400 <BR>&gt; and up, and that doesn't count parts/seals, or input bearings/clutch <BR>&gt; fork.<BR>&gt; Question is, what do I look for, watch out for when dropping the <BR>&gt; cradle? what troublespots are there? can I drop the cradle and rear <BR>&gt; struts as a unit?...saw a wrecking yard here do it with the exhaust <BR>&gt; still attached..is that feasible?  I have the Helms, but not real <BR>&gt; clear on procedure.  I wish I could get the car to the FieroFactory, <BR>&gt; then it would get done right.  I have air impacts, and most <BR>&gt; equipment, but the arthritis in my hands acts up pretty bad <BR>&gt; anymore...<BR>&gt; Honestly, is this doable in a person's garage...am I losing it?  <BR>&gt; This is a pretty nice GT and really don't want to give it up without <BR>&gt; a fighting chance...Thanks for all your help and expertise in the <BR>&gt; past... &gt; Jim and Jan &gt; Yuma,AZ &gt; Calvin<BR>&gt; Bits and Pieces &gt; and the new Red Gt(87) <BR>||||reg||6
